#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Meowdoro remote helper: sends what Claude Code and Codex are doing on this machine to Meowdoro on your Mac.

    agent hook ──▶ `meowdoro-remote hook <agent>` ──unix socket──▶ `meowdoro-remote daemon` ──HTTPS──▶ relay ──▶ Meowdoro

- hook:   called by the agent's hooks. Keeps only status fields (never prompts, tool inputs or outputs, replies),
          hands them to the daemon and exits at once. Never prints, always exits 0, so it can't disturb the agent.
- daemon: started by the first hook and gone ten minutes after the last session closes. Reads how full each
          session's context is (and Codex's plan limits) from the end of its transcript and adds only those
          numbers to the event. Encrypts each event (AES-256-GCM, key from the setup command; the relay only
          sees ciphertext), sends it, retries, checks every 15 s that the agents are still running and whether
          a finished session was read in Claude Desktop or ChatGPT here, and sends a heartbeat every minute
          (with which agents have the plugin, for the Mac's Settings).
- setup:  `meowdoro-remote setup <relay-url> <code>` adds that Meowdoro's relay, channel and key to the ones this
          machine sends to (each Mac's line adds its Mac; the same line again replaces it), then asks Claude Code
          and Codex to install the helper's plugin, meowdoro-remote (their own commands). One plugin, however many
          Macs: the daemon seals each event with each Mac's key. `list` shows them, `remove <number>` drops one,
          `uninstall` removes everything. A Meowdoro running on this machine too keeps its own plugin, meowdoro:
          each hook goes to both.

Python 3.8+, standard library only. Files: ~/.meowdoro/ (remote.json, remote.sock, remote.lock, remote.log, agent-plugins/).
"""

